openmediavault-sunburstfs (7.0~phase5-1) stable; urgency=low

  * Fixed: the placeholder ("chart not generated yet") page rendered as
    raw JSON instead of HTML in the browser, while the real cached chart
    rendered fine. Root cause: the cache-hit file always ends in .html
    (renamed by the refresh script), but the placeholder served a bare
    tempnam() file with no extension at all -- download.php's
    Content-Type detection turned out to key off the file extension,
    not only the RPC response's own declared headers. Fixed by giving
    the placeholder file a real .html extension too.
  * Test coverage: added the one real gap found on review --
    SunburstConfig::cachePathFor() (the single source of truth linking
    what the refresh script writes to what getPage() reads) had zero
    coverage despite its extension already having silently changed
    once. Everything else untested is legitimately outer-ring/framework
    glue (RPC calls, file I/O, CLI entrypoint), consistent with this
    project's Circle Architecture approach throughout.
  * Packaging: Section corrected from net to admin (this is a storage
    utility, not a networking one). The package build now runs the full
    PHPUnit suite via debian/rules (override_dh_auto_test, respecting
    DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nocheck) and refuses to produce a .deb if any
    test fails -- Build-Depends gained phpunit accordingly.
  * Trimmed verbose inline comments across source and packaging files
    down to the essential why; class/method docblocks kept as-is.
  * Added README.md (install/uninstall/dev instructions) and
    Taskfile.yml (test/build/install/clean convenience commands) --
    debian/rules itself stays a Makefile, since dpkg-buildpackage
    requires that specifically.

 -- openmediavault-sunburstfs plugin <chutchens91@gmail.com>  Thu, 13 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

openmediavault-sunburstfs (7.0~phase4-1) stable; urgency=low

  * Rendering rewrite, prompted by live feedback on the deployed
    server-rendered PNG: rings existed but were illegible (no labels,
    colors only varied by depth so a filesystem dominated by one long
    single-child folder chain -- a real deployed example -- rendered as
    a flat "bullseye"), the legend only ever covered 3 root-level
    entries, and a raster image couldn't scale cleanly across devices.
    - New approach: the chart is now a standalone HTML page rendered
      client-side with D3 (vendored, no CDN), served through the same
      download.php + RPC `filepath` + explicit Content-Type mechanism
      already proven for the PNG, just with `text/html` instead of
      `image/png`. This is a genuinely separate document -- not passed
      through OMV's Angular sanitizeHtml pipe -- so it can run real
      JavaScript despite OMV7's declarative workbench having no
      supported extension point for that (confirmed earlier in this
      project); the view page now links out to it (`<a href>`,
      target="_blank") instead of embedding an `<img>`.
    - D3's own well-known "zoomable sunburst" pattern replaces all of
      SunburstWedgeLayout, HslColor, TextFitter, and SunburstGdRenderer
      (deleted, along with their tests) -- color-by-branch-family,
      adaptive label visibility, and click-to-zoom drill-down (deferred
      early in this project specifically because it seemed to need
      client JS OMV couldn't run) all come from D3's reference
      implementation rather than hand-rolled PHP, which is exactly the
      code that had been buggy across several iterations already (the
      GD degenerate-arc bug, legend overlap, the bullseye colors).
    - New SunburstPageTemplate (test-first): the one piece of real
      logic left, injecting the tree as JSON into the page template.
      Deliberately escapes with JSON_HEX_TAG|JSON_HEX_AMP, tested
      directly against a directory name containing "</script>" -- a
      real, not hypothetical, break-out-of-the-script-block risk once
      untrusted directory names end up inside a <script> block.
    - New SunburstPageGenerator replaces SunburstChartGenerator. New
      outside-in integration test (real scanner + builder + template)
      exists specifically to catch wiring/field-name mismatches between
      the pieces -- the same class of bug that once slipped through
      here as an incomplete branch -> siblingIndex rename.
    - D3 (v7.9.0, ISC licensed) is vendored as its own untouched file
      (templates/d3.v7.min.js), spliced into the page template at
      generation time rather than pre-merged into one file, specifically
      so debian/copyright can attribute it accurately as a single
      unambiguous file rather than a mix of GPL-3+ and ISC content in
      one blob.
    - debian/control no longer depends on php-gd or fonts-dejavu-core --
      nothing server-side renders anything any more.
    - Reviewed live locally before any NAS deployment: a real HTML file
      generated through the actual production pipeline (real
      SunburstTreeBuilder + SunburstPageTemplate, real du numbers from
      the deployed all_data-dominated filesystem) was opened in a
      browser and confirmed working -- labels, branch colors, hover
      tooltips, and click-to-zoom all correct -- before packaging this
      version.

 -- openmediavault-sunburstfs plugin <chutchens91@gmail.com>  Thu, 13 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

openmediavault-sunburstfs (7.0~phase3-9) stable; urgency=low

  * Root cause found (not just mitigated) for the two system crashes:
    confirmed via `omv-rpc FileSystemMgmt enumerateMountedFilesystems`
    directly that the OS root filesystem ("/") is a real entry in the
    mounted-filesystem list on this system (it's a normal managed
    mountpoint here, not synthesized), it had never been successfully
    cached, and StalestFilesystemPicker always prioritizes a
    never-cached filesystem over any real cache age -- so root was
    picked every single unattended run. `du` walking root (including
    e.g. Docker's per-layer storage under /var/lib/docker, which can
    hold a pathologically large *directory count* while using little
    actual space) produced output large enough that buffering it via
    exec() exhausted system memory. Confirmed the box's baseline
    memory usage is normally low -- this wasn't a marginal system
    tipped over by one contributing factor, our scan was the entire
    cause.
    - New ManagedFilesystemFilter (test-first): excludes the root
      filesystem from every code path (picker, manual argv[1]
      override) -- root isn't useful for this plugin's purpose anyway
      (finding what to move off data disks, not charting the OS drive).
    - New hard cap (test-first, confirmed fails without the fix and
      passes with it): DuDiskUsageScanner now pipes `du`'s output
      through `head -n 500000` (SunburstConfig::MAX_DIRECTORY_ENTRIES).
      `du` prints deepest-first, so the scanned path's own summary
      line is always last -- truncation therefore always self-detects
      via SunburstTreeBuilder's existing "root path not present" check,
      no new detection code needed (confirmed the ordering directly).
      This bounds memory for *any* filesystem with a pathological
      directory count, not just this specific one.
    - Timer manually disabled during investigation
      (systemctl disable --now); needs to be manually re-enabled once
      this fix is confirmed stable.

 -- openmediavault-sunburstfs plugin <chutchens91@gmail.com>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

openmediavault-sunburstfs (7.0~phase3-8) stable; urgency=low

  * Major reliability response to a real incident: a NAS already
    running close to its memory ceiling (its own other services --
    minio, several Docker containers, clamav, kopia) crashed and
    required a reboot; timing correlated with sustained background
    `du` activity from refreshing every mounted filesystem on a fixed
    15min timer regardless of how long any individual scan took.
    - New StalestFilesystemPicker (test-first): each run now refreshes
      only the single filesystem whose cached chart has gone longest
      without a fresh one (or was never generated), not every mounted
      filesystem. Spreads the same total work out instead of
      compounding it.
    - Timer interval widened 15min -> 1h (OnActiveSec/OnUnitActiveSec),
      RandomizedDelaySec 120 -> 300. Service TimeoutStartSec tightened
      6h -> 2h30m now that a run is bounded by one filesystem's
      SCAN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS (2h) plus rendering, not several in
      sequence.

  * Color scheme: siblings within a branch were only distinguished by
    depth-based lightness (all shades of one inherited hue), which
    confirmed by testing (a chart where an entire 11TB disk lived
    under one top-level folder) makes the actionable detail -- what's
    actually big -- invisible, since that's usually below depth 0.
    SunburstWedgeLayout's `branch` (inherited from the depth-0
    ancestor) replaced with `siblingIndex` (recomputed fresh at every
    level, own position among own parent's children -- test-first,
    confirmed the old inheritance behavior fails the new test and the
    new behavior passes it). Every ring now gets its own hue based on
    its own position among siblings, not an inherited one.
    - Found and fixed a real wiring bug from this rename via a new
      end-to-end test (real SunburstWedgeLayout -> real
      SunburstGdRenderer, not hand-built wedge arrays): the renderer
      still read the old `branch` key after the rename, silently
      collapsing every wedge to the same color (missing array key ->
      null -> null % N == 0) -- confirmed the hand-built-wedge tests
      alone couldn't catch this, since they used whichever key name
      the renderer itself expected rather than what the real producer
      emits. Confirmed the new end-to-end test fails without the fix
      and passes with it.

 -- openmediavault-sunburstfs plugin <chutchens91@gmail.com>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

openmediavault-sunburstfs (7.0~phase3-7) stable; urgency=low

  * Visual bugs found from the first real rendered chart on live data:
    - Legend name/size text overlap: truncation was by fixed character
      count, not measured pixel width, so a long-but-under-the-cap
      name still visually collided with its own row's size text.
      Fixed with a new TextFitter (test-first, fake width-measurer)
      wired to real font metrics in the renderer; each row's available
      name width is now derived from that row's actual measured size
      text width, not a guess.
    - Every wedge silently rendered as the same gray: confirmed by a
      faithful reproduction of the real draw sequence that GD's
      imagefilledarc() treats a degenerate (rounded start == rounded
      end) arc as a full 360° circle, not "nothing" -- a wedge so
      thin its angle rounds to zero (e.g. a few hundred bytes next to
      a multi-GB sibling) was overwriting every previously-drawn
      wedge. New regression test (SunburstGdRendererTest) renders and
      samples actual output pixels; confirmed it fails without the
      fix before confirming it passes with it.
    - Chart looked small inside an oversized default hint/alert-panel
      container: added a responsive style attribute to the <img> tag
      (degrades gracefully to previous behavior if sanitizeHtml strips
      it) and bumped the native render resolution (600->760).

 -- openmediavault-sunburstfs plugin <chutchens91@gmail.com>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

openmediavault-sunburstfs (7.0~phase3-6) stable; urgency=low

  * Two bugs found by actually running the refresh script on the real
    NAS, not discoverable in this sandbox (no OMV framework/systemd
    available locally):
    - "RPC service 'FileSystemMgmt' not found": Rpc::call() defaults
      to MODE_LOCAL, which only works from inside omv-engined itself.
      OmvFilesystemDirectory is only ever used from the standalone
      refresh CLI script -- confirmed via OMV's own omv-rpc CLI tool
      source that MODE_REMOTE must be passed explicitly for a
      standalone process to reach the real daemon over its socket.
      Also switched the script's bootstrap from (autoloader, env,
      functions) to (autoloader, globals, functions), matching
      omv-rpc's proven pattern instead of download.php's (a different
      execution context).
    - Timer showed "Trigger: n/a" after install -- would never have
      fired on its own. OnUnitActiveSec alone has no anchor until the
      service has completed at least once; added OnActiveSec (anchors
      to the timer's own activation) for a real first run ~15min
      after install.

 -- openmediavault-sunburstfs plugin <chutchens91@gmail.com>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

openmediavault-sunburstfs (7.0~phase3-5) stable; urgency=low

  * Fault-tolerance hardening, requested explicitly after an nginx
    outage during testing (root cause: an unrelated port-443 conflict
    on the NAS, not this plugin's code -- but our repeated package
    installs plausibly triggered the nginx restart that surfaced it):
    - New TimeoutBoundedProcess (test-first, tests/Integration/Sunburst/
      TimeoutBoundedProcessTest.php proves the kill actually happens
      via a real `sleep`, not just assumed from the `timeout` docs).
      DuDiskUsageScanner now depends on it instead of building an
      unbounded `du` command directly -- a stuck NFS mount or failing
      drive can no longer hold a filesystem's lock forever.
    - openmediavault-sunburstfs.service: Nice=19,
      IOSchedulingClass=best-effort so a background scan never
      competes with real NAS usage.
    - openmediavault-sunburstfs.timer: RandomizedDelaySec=120 jitter.
    - getImage(): the cache-read/placeholder path is now wrapped so an
      unexpected failure (corrupt cache file, GD hiccup) always still
      returns a valid placeholder image instead of a raw RPC error.
      Missing fsUuid still throws directly -- that's a caller error,
      not a NAS-health concern.
    - omv-sunburstfs-refresh: top-level try/catch so nothing can crash
      it uncaught mid-run, on top of the existing per-filesystem
      isolation.

 -- openmediavault-sunburstfs plugin <chutchens91@gmail.com>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

openmediavault-sunburstfs (7.0~phase3-4) stable; urgency=low

  * Major: getImage() ran `du` synchronously inside the RPC request.
    Confirmed by testing this can take long enough on a large
    filesystem to hit the nginx proxy timeout (504) AND get
    omv-engined SIGKILLed by its own service watchdog -- taking down
    all RPC functionality on the box, not just this chart, and
    possibly contributing to a full system hang. getImage() is now a
    pure cache read (instant, no scanning, no timeout risk at all).
    Actual generation moved to a new background script
    (usr/sbin/omv-sunburstfs-refresh), run on a systemd timer
    (openmediavault-sunburstfs.timer, 15min interval, deliberately no
    OnBootSec so it can't fire immediately on install), with
    per-filesystem flock locking so a long scan of one large
    filesystem just holds its lock across however many ticks it
    takes, never overlapping itself and never blocking other
    filesystems. Shows a placeholder image until the first background
    run completes for a given filesystem.

  * Legend + branch-colored chart (was flat 4-color-by-depth):
    SunburstWedgeLayout now carries a `branch` index per wedge (which
    top-level entry it descends from); new HslColor (unit tested) and
    a curated hue palette give each top-level branch a consistent
    color family across depths; SunburstGdRenderer now also draws the
    formatted total size centered in the hole and a side legend
    (colored dot + name + size) built directly from tree['children'],
    reusing binary_format() for consistency with the rest of the OMV
    UI. Requires fonts-dejavu-core (added to Depends).

  * Removed FilesystemMountPointResolver: dead code after this
    redesign -- getImage() no longer resolves a single fsUuid (pure
    cache read), and the refresh script iterates every mounted
    filesystem rather than looking one up.

 -- openmediavault-sunburstfs plugin <chutchens91@gmail.com>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

openmediavault-sunburstfs (7.0~phase3-3) stable; urgency=low

  * fsUuid resolution threw a database XPath error -- confirmed by
    testing that a filesystem's own uuid (from
    FileSystemMgmt.enumerateMountedFilesystems) and the
    conf.system.filesystem.mountpoint config object's own primary key
    are different UUID namespaces, not the same value. Extracted the
    lookup into FilesystemMountPointResolver (pure, unit tested, 3
    new tests) wrapping a FilesystemDirectoryInterface abstraction
    (OmvFilesystemDirectory concrete impl calls Rpc::call the same
    RPC the picker page already uses, matching on uuid to get
    mountpoint) instead of the wrong config-database assumption.

